"""Content A: Threshold and Worlds.
Tuple formats:
  ("lore", kicker, title, [paras], prompt)
  ("journal", kicker, prompt, lines)
  ("tracker", kicker, prompt, [row labels])
Every lore page's last para is its Where-this-comes-from line.
"""

CH1 = [
("lore", "Threshold", "Who Were the Old Norse",
 ["The people in this book are the Old Norse. They lived around the North Sea and the North Atlantic, mostly from about 750 to 1050 CE, a period called the Viking Age. They were not one kingdom with one border. They lived on farms, islands, and fjords. The sea was their main road, and they named sea routes the way we name highways. Austrvegr, for example, meant the east-way. They spoke North Germanic. You can read their language in runes, letters cut into stone, starting around the second century. Ships, trade, and raids tied Norway, Denmark, and Sweden to Iceland and beyond. Picture a blue-grey stone with lichen growing in its cuts. Its words are short. They tell you who raised it and for whom.",
  "Stones and burials show what people left in the ground. Their inscriptions can be dated to when they were made. Books tell longer stories. The Poetic Edda and Snorri Sturluson’s Prose Edda are collections of old poems and myths. Icelandic sagas are prose stories about families. Icelandic scribes copied many of them centuries later. So you have two kinds of source. Stones are one window. You look at what was left in the ground. Books are the other window. You look at what was written on the page. I will tell you which window you are looking through each time.",
  "The sea was their road, and the stone was their record. One man raised a blue-grey slab for his brother and cut the names deep so lichen would not hide them. The Poetic Edda kept what stones could not hold: voices, quarrels, and jokes told by lamplight. Today you live with both kinds of record. Stones keep what people finished. Stories keep what people cared about. When you read, hold both in mind.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The language, dates, and stones are known from runestones. The Poetic Edda and Snorri’s Prose Edda were copied down centuries later."],
 "Tonight, spend half an hour at your table. Read one short passage, then write whether it is a stone record or a story record. Write three lines and date them today."),
("lore", "Threshold", "Who Were the Old Norse",
 ["This book has clear limits. Know them before you give it your time. No Viking wrote a handbook of belief for us to find. The Roman writer Tacitus described Germanic peoples from outside the frontier. He wrote for Rome, with taxes and wars on his mind. Centuries later, Icelandic Christians copied the Poetic Edda and Icelandic sagas onto vellum, a prepared animal skin. They saved the voices, but they changed the frame. The Arab traveler Ibn Fadlan watched a burial on the Volga and wrote it down as an outsider. The record has gaps. This book says so openly.",
  "This book is not Celtic lore with Norse names, and it is not Anglo-Saxon lore either. Neighboring peoples shared some trade and some words, but their customs differed. Nor is this a modern mix pretending to be old. Where modern people filled gaps, I say so plainly, and I say when a practice was rebuilt from clues. Where old stones or the Poetic Edda speak, I name the source. Where later farm custom may carry an older shape, I say that too. You will find a note called “Where this comes from” at the foot of each lore page.",
  "Tacitus wrote about Germanic peoples from outside the frontier, for Rome. Ibn Fadlan watched a burial on the Volga and wrote for strangers. Icelandic scribes copied old voices by lamplight and changed the frame as they saved the pages. You can still work with these sources carefully. Ask who wrote each one and why. Then you will know how far to trust it.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> How this book works. Its method is modern, but it is built on old sources."],
 "Write one sentence for what you hope this book will give you, and one for what you hope it will leave out. Write both tonight and date them today."),
("lore", "Threshold", "Who Were the Old Norse",
 ["Start with the body you bring to this book. The Hávamál (Havamal), a wisdom poem in the Poetic Edda, values a clear head over a high mood. It tells a traveler to keep his wits about him at the table. It tells him to listen before he speaks, and to eat before he gives advice. Picture a cold morning at the door: boots on, breath showing, shoulders dropped. No one in the Hávamál tries to find wisdom while hungry or half asleep. They begin fed, wearing shoes, and awake.",
  "The practice here is simple. Take three slow breaths and feel your feet on the floor before you start the day’s work. This is a modern exercise, not an old rite. It asks you to notice what is loud in your body before you decide anything. The Hávamál prefers a calm traveler. Hunger and rush make judgment worse.",
  "Picture your boots by the door and your breath in the cold air. Let your shoulders drop as your feet find the floor and your weight settles evenly. Do the same each morning, before your phone or inbox takes your attention. Many people find they think more clearly after a slow start. Test this for yourself.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The counsel is from the Hávamál in the Poetic Edda. The three-breath practice was written for this book."],
 "Take three slow breaths tonight, with your feet on the floor. Name which part feels loudest: body, attention, or intent. Give it three lines and date them today."),
("journal", "Threshold", "Which part is loud right now? Name it and say what it wants.", 13),
("journal", "Threshold", "Noticing log, day one. Go to a spot where you can watch and listen. Date it. Write three things you see and three you hear.", 14),
]

CH2 = [
("lore", "Nine Realms", "Creation: fire meets mist",
 ["Before anything had a name, there was a gap. The Old Norse called it Ginnungagap, which probably means something like \"the yawning gap.\" Scholars are not sure what the first part of the word means, so treat this translation as a best guess. The gap was not a who. It was a where: a huge empty space with fire on one side and ice on the other. Múspell (Muspell), the fire world, burned at one edge, and the giant Surtr (Surt) guarded it with a flaming sword. Niflheimr (Niflheim), the mist world, lay on the other side, where poisonous rivers froze into thick layers of frost. Nothing lived in either place. The gap waited, and the power of this book begins in that waiting: when opposites meet, something new can be made.",
  "Then the meeting of fire and ice began to make the world. Sparks flew out of Múspell and crossed the gap. They met the frost of Niflheimr, and the ice melted into drops. Those drops took shape as Ymir, the first of the giants. A cow named Auðumla (Audumla) fed on the salty ice until Búri (Buri) appeared, the grandfather of Óðinn (Odin). Óðinn and his brothers killed Ymir and made the world from his body: earth from his flesh, sea from his blood, sky from his skull, mountains from his bones, and trees from his hair. No one spoke this world into being. Heat met frost in an empty place, and life came out of that meeting.",
  "In Snorri Sturluson’s Gylfaginning (the tricking of Gylfi), three gods found two fallen trees on a shore, Ask and Embla, ash and elm. The gods gave them breath, wit, warmth, and color, and the trees stood up as the first people. Today you can take a smaller version of this. When you bake bread or mend a seam, you repeat that first making. Your hands put care into plain material.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The creation story is from the Völuspá (Voluspa), Vafþrúðnismál (Vafthrudnismal), and Grímnismál (Grimnismal) in the Poetic Edda, and from Snorri’s Prose Edda. The household use was written for this book."],
 "What are you building with your hands this season? Name the project, its stage, and its next step. Give it three dated lines."),
("lore", "Nine Realms", "Nine Realms: sky, middle, and wild",
 ["The Poetic Edda describes nine realms, or worlds. They are not stacked like floors. They are arranged around one great tree, Yggdrasill (Yggdrasil), which connects them. Ásgarðr (Asgard) is the high home of the Æsir (Aesir) gods, such as Óðinn and Þórr. It lies in the sky and is reached by the rainbow bridge Bifröst (Bifrost). Miðgarðr (Midgard) is the middle realm where people live. Óðinn and his brothers made it from the body of the giant Ymir. A great serpent, Jörmungandr (Jormungandr), coils in the sea around its edge.",
  "Vanaheimr (Vanaheim) is the fertile realm of the Vanir, an older family of gods tied to harvest, weather, and plenty. Njörðr (Njord), Freyr, and Freyja belong to them. Jötunheimr (Jotunheim) is the wild land of the jötnar (giants), who are linked with chaos and untamed nature. The Æsir and the giants are often at odds in the Poetic Edda. The Poetic Edda names these homes. The ring-shaped diagram is a modern way to draw them. Neither the Poetic Edda nor Snorri Sturluson’s Prose Edda gives a single map.",
  "Use the realms to check your week. The Æsir bring oath and order. The Vanir bring harvest and plenty. The giants bring the wild edges, which you cannot fully control. Each one is needed. Ask which realm your week lives in most, and which one it is missing.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The realms and Bifröst are named in the Poetic Edda and Snorri’s Prose Edda. The ring-shaped map was drawn for this book."],
 "Look at your week and name the realm it lives in most: order, harvest, wild edges, or something else. Write three dated lines: one on that realm, and two on where your week is missing one."),
("lore", "Nine Realms", "Nine Realms: light, dark, fire, ice, and the dead",
 ["Álfheimr (Alfheim) is the bright realm of the ljósálfar (light elves), who are linked with beauty, art, and craft. Svartálfaheimr (Svartalfheim), also called Niðavellir (Nidavellir) in some sources, is the underground home of the dwarfs and dark elves. Its smiths forged the finest treasures for the gods. Their skill shows in what they made, not in how they looked.",
  "Múspell is the realm of fire, heat, and glowing embers, ruled by the fire giant Surtr. Niflheim is the realm of deep ice, cold fog, and frost. At its center lies the spring Hvergelmir, and one of the roots of Yggdrasill reaches down to it. The two realms stand on opposite sides of the gap. Their meeting made the world.",
  "Hel is the dark realm of the dead, ruled by the giantess Hel. She receives those who die of old age, sickness, or causes that are not heroic in battle, according to Snorri Sturluson’s Prose Edda. This book does not describe the road there. Here the practice is simpler: keep the names of your dead in use, as you do on the Ancestors pages.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> These realms are named in the Poetic Edda and Snorri’s Prose Edda. The name Niðavellir and the sorting of elves into light and dark come from later sources."],
 "Name one thing in your life that shines, one that is cold or dark, and one that burns. Write a dated line for each. Give it three dated lines."),
("lore", "Nine Realms", "Yggdrasill: the ash that holds",
 ["Yggdrasill is the great ash tree in the Poetic Edda and Snorri Sturluson’s Prose Edda, and it holds the worlds together. Its branches stretch so far that the Poetic Edda never fully describes them. Its three roots reach three wells: Hvergelmir in Niflheim, Mimir’s well, and the well of Urðr (Urd), where the Norns sit. A stag feeds on its shoots above. At its root sit the Nornir (Norns), women who shape fate. The squirrel Ratatoskr (Ratatosk) runs messages up and down the trunk. He scolds both ends as he goes.",
  "Read this as a picture of how things connect, not as a road map. The Poetic Edda and Prose Edda name the tree and its residents. But those copies are late, so the source is solid but not early. No source describes a home ritual for the tree, so this book does not invent one. What you can take home is simpler. Everything depends on something else. Roots need water. News travels the trunk like gossip. You can only tend what you can reach. The top of the tree can have its own weather.",
  "The Nornir sit at the root and shape what comes next. Your life works the same way. What you feed at the roots shows up later in the leaves. Give your roots steady care, such as sleep, regular meals, and honest talk. Results come in time. Notice which roots are dry this week, and water one of them on purpose.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The tree and its tenants are described in the Poetic Edda and Snorri’s Prose Edda."],
 "Draw your life as a tree tonight, with roots, trunk, and crown. Mark three roots that feed you and two branches you tend. Name one voice that scolds you, like Ratatoskr. Date it with today’s date."),
("lore", "Nine Realms", "Where the dead go: Valhöll (Valhalla), Fólkvangr (Folkvangr), Hel",
 ["The dead do not all go to one place, according to the Poetic Edda. Valhöll is Óðinn’s hall. It takes half of those slain in battle. It is roofed with shields and raftered with spears. Freyja takes the other half to Fólkvangr, her own field. Hel keeps those who die of sickness or old age in her fenced hall. Her hall is neither burning nor shining. A grim shore is set aside for oath-breakers and murderers. Baldr went down to Hel, and he stayed there after the ransom failed.",
  "Each claim has its own source. That makes the tangle easier to sort out. Valhöll and Fólkvangr appear in the Poetic Edda, so they are named there. Hel’s hall appears in Snorri Sturluson’s Prose Edda and in the Poetic Edda. Both were written down late. Burial mounds outside the stories tell a plainer story. They hold grave goods, ship settings, and feasting gear. These show care for the journey. Stories and mound earth are two kinds of witness. Neither one cancels the other, and you can hold both while you grieve.",
  "Your memory of someone is a form of care. It can last longer than any set of beliefs. You do not need the right belief to honor them. You need to keep their name in use. Speak it, write it, and keep one thing they loved in your home.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Valhöll, Fólkvangr, and Hel are described in the Poetic Edda and Snorri’s Prose Edda, both copied down late. The mounds are known from archaeology."],
 "Name one person who is gone and one thing they cared about. Name the place you picture them resting. Give it three plain lines with today’s date."),
("lore", "Nine Realms", "Ragnarok: winter without spring",
 ["The end of the world, Ragnarok, follows the order in the Völuspá. First comes Fimbulwinter, three winters in a row with no summer between. Family members break their promises to each other, and an age of axes begins. The sun darkens and the stars fall. Naglfar, the ship made of dead men’s nails, floats free. Heimdallr (Heimdall) sounds the horn. The gods ride to Vígríðr (Vigrid) plain, a hundred leagues every way. Óðinn falls to the wolf. Þórr (Thor) kills the serpent and falls nine steps after. Loki and Heimdallr kill each other. Fire takes all.",
  "The poem does not stop at the end. Earth rises green from the sea a second time. Baldr and Höðr (Hodr) come back to Óðinn’s victory hall. Víðarr (Vidar) and Váli (Vali) live on. The golden game pieces are found again in the grass where the gods once played. The Völuspá tells both the doom and the new green. Snorri Sturluson’s Prose Edda retells the story later, in plain prose. The poem suggests that an ending can be followed by new growth. This book offers no fearful ritual for the end of the world, and it does not ask you to fear it.",
  "When something in your life ends, look for what grows after it. Name the ending. Then find one small sign of new growth. Notice what comes back, and tend it. You can grieve the loss and still look for new growth. Write down what you see, and look again in a week.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The doom and the greening are from the Völuspá, retold in Snorri’s Prose Edda. This book offers no rites for the end of the world."],
 "Name one ending in your life. Name what grew after it, even something small. Give it three dated lines and read them aloud once."),
("lore", "Nine Realms", "Doors: mounds, mist, sea-fog",
 ["Many stories say you cross into other worlds at edges, not at centers. In Icelandic story, burial mounds are said to stand open at Yule, the midwinter feast, and the dead walk near the living. In these stories, a mound, the shore, and feast night all mark a place where one world opens into another.",
  "Read this as a story pattern first, not a literal gate. Mounds mark crossing points in Icelandic sagas, which is a repeated story pattern. The Yule opening of the mounds comes mostly from later Icelandic folk belief. Some farms still leave certain hills and landing places alone. That is folk custom, related to the stories but separate from them. Your own street can feel different at dusk. That feeling is real, but it is your own, recorded today. It is not proof about other worlds.",
  "Use the pattern as a way to pay attention. Go out at dusk and notice the edges near you: a gate, a water line, a crossing. Write down what changes at each one. Noticing these spots slows you down and helps you see your street more clearly.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Mounds as doors appear in Icelandic sagas. The idea that they open at Yule comes from later Icelandic folk belief."],
 "Walk your block for ten minutes tonight and pick three edge spots. Give each spot one line for what changes there. Read the three aloud and circle the one that feels strongest. Date them with today’s date."),
("lore", "Nine Realms", "Guest rules: Hávamál manners",
 ["In the Hávamál, guests and hosts live by house rules, and the rules are clear. The Hávamál tells a traveler to watch before he sits down. It tells him to eat early, speak little, and listen long. It tells him never to mock a guest or a host. In the songs, oath-breakers lose their good name. The host feeds first.",
  "These rules make sense as household habits first and as scholarship second. The Hávamál does teach guest rules. Folk custom keeps related rules in Yule peace and threshold greetings. They are related, not the same. Today, turn the rules around. You are the host in your own home, and your home tells visitors what to expect.",
  "The Hávamál tells a traveler to watch before he sits. Oath-breakers lost what the songs gave them. The host feeds first. Your own table can follow that rule: no guest should leave hungry. Make that a habit.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The guest rules are from the Hávamál."],
 "Write three rules you would post for tired guests. Keep each one short enough for an index card. Tape the card inside a cupboard and read it each day for a week. Date it with today’s date."),
("lore", "Nine Realms", "Crooked time: promises across winter",
 ["The Poetic Edda and Icelandic sagas rarely run in a straight line from birth to ending, the way modern novels often do. The Völuspá does not move in a straight line. The seeress remembers forward into doom, then past it into green earth rising again. Oaths sworn at Yule were meant to hold people through the summer fights. A vow spoken drunk on a winter night comes back sober at the spring turn. Egill (Egil) in Egils saga is another example, moving from grief to verse.",
  "This crooked pattern is simple, not mystical. The Völuspá loops back and hints at the future. That is a known literary shape. Farm life was counted in winters. That appears in Icelandic sagas and in Grágás (the Grey Goose Laws), where men counted their age in the winters they had kept. Your own year already works this way. School terms and work rotas set the pace, and anniversaries arrive before you feel ready. Many people find that winter feels easier once they stop fighting it.",
  "Use this on purpose. Pick one promise you made this winter and check it against the spring. Ask whether it still stands, and what it asks of you in the coming season. A written promise is easier to keep than a remembered one. Put a reminder on your calendar for the first warm day of spring, the day the promise comes due.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The looping style is in the Völuspá and Icelandic sagas, and winter-counting is in the sagas."],
 "Pick one promise you made this winter and write it tonight in pencil. Write what you agreed to, what spring must bring, and what autumn must close. Fold the page and keep it where you get dressed. Date it."),
("lore", "Nine Realms", "Neither-times: dawn and dusk",
 ["Dawn and dusk, the half-light times, appear in many old stories. Your own street can show you why they feel special. In Icelandic sagas, people meet at the shore, where the tide meets the sand. Messages come in the moment between sleeping and waking. In Icelandic sagas, dreams guide both voyages and killings. Folk custom still honors these in-between times. Some families keep a Yule-night watch and draw water at dawn.",
  "Keep the layers separate. Dream warnings appear often in Icelandic sagas. It is a literary motif, a story device that repeats. It is not proof that anyone kept dream books. Later folk dream dictionaries exist, but this book does not use them. This book does not decode dream symbols.",
  "Keep a short watch at dawn or dusk. Stand at one window or step outside for five minutes, and notice the light and the sounds. Noticing the change between day and night slows you down and trains your attention.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Dream warnings appear as a repeated device in Icelandic sagas. The dawn-and-dusk watch was written for this book."],
 "Choose dawn or dusk tomorrow. Stand at one window or step outside for five minutes, and write what you see and hear. Give it one dated line."),
]
